WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:06.060
When the Netflix original film The Platform
dropped in February 2020, the Spanish horror-thriller

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.190
quickly generated a ton of buzz for its unique
dystopian setting and the prescient symbolism

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.769
at the core of the narrative.

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:16.880
At first glance, the film appears to be a
story of individual greed and the struggle

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.610
to survive, but as the movie reaches its haunting
conclusion, it becomes clear that there's

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.040
something deeper going on.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.170
At the outset of The Platform, we're introduced
to Goreng, a new inmate in a vertical prison.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.870
At the top, a team of chefs prepare a lavish
feast big enough to feed everyone in the hundreds

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:37.770
of cells below, and each day, the titular
platform containing the food is lowered.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:42.100
The platform stops at each cell for two minutes,
and the two prisoners in that cell can eat

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.190
as much or as little as they want.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.149
Of course, those at the top get their fill,
while those at the bottom are left with nothing

00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:49.499
and end up starving.

00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:53.579
The catch is that the prisoners are assigned
a new cell each month, rearranging the pecking

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:55.460
order in relation to the platform.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.559
The ambiguous ending of The Platform and the
film's metaphorical nature throughout have

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.319
left many viewers trying to figure out what
it all means.

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:06.220
Let's run through the symbolism of the movie
and what message the filmmakers wanted us

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.500
to take away from the final moments.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.280
We get bits of details about what the purpose
of the prison is throughout, but it's not

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.110
until Goreng meets Imoguiri, who worked for
the prison herself before becoming an inmate,

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.980
that we get a more concrete idea.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.290
Imoguiri tells Goreng that the administration
prefers to refer to the pit-like prison as

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.560
a Vertical Self-Management Center, or VSC.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.350
She explains that in order for the food to
reach all the way down to the lowest levels

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.960
there needs to be a "spontaneous sense of
solidarity", meaning everyone, without communicating

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.550
to one another, only takes as much as they
need.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:41.340
She seems to imply that the whole purpose
of the prison is to create a system in which

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.750
people can learn to foster this spontaneous
sense of solidarity.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.140
However, Goreng proposes a more sinister interpretation:

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:53.700
"If that solidarity emerged, they would know
to prevent it happening on the outside, wouldn't

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.700
they?"

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.780
According to Goreng's interpretation of the
prison, it's a small-scale model of a harsh

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.530
capitalist system that the ruling class is
using to test the responses of the proletariat.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.050
It's a tool for the rulers, not society as
a whole.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:11.480
Most of the main characters we meet in The
Platform represent archetypes or ideas larger

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.480
than themselves.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.230
For instance, Goreng, who volunteers to enter
the pit in order to obtain a college diploma,

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:18.660
represents an idealisic intellectual.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.870
At the beginning of the film, he shames the
greed of those who take more than their share

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:26.870
when they have the opportunity, but doesn't
understand why they do it, as he has yet to

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.090
spend a month at the bottom of the pit.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.750
Similarly, Imoguiri represents the willfully
ignorant bureaucrats upon which a system like

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.840
the VSC rely in order to function.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.500
When Imoguiri enters the pit, it's revealed
that she's mostly in the dark as to what exactly

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.620
happens in there.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.570
Like Goreng, she attempts to convince the
prisoners below her to ration their food,

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.140
but they don't listen to her pleas.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.290
Perhaps the most enigmatic character in The
Platform is Miharu, a mute woman who rides

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.470
the platform each month in search of her young
child.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.470
Miharu is disdained by the fellow prisoners
and is often attacked.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:02.040
Even Imoguiri derides her struggle, claiming
that her search for her child is a lie because

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.810
nobody under 16 is allowed in the pit.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.880
Miharu represents those who suffer on the
periphery of society, whose stories we don't

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.321
want to believe because they sound so cruel
that we can't imagine that we live in the

00:03:12.321 --> 00:03:17.270
same system, for instance, people experiencing
homelessness or other forms of extreme poverty.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:21.430
At the end of the film, we learn that Miharu's
child is real, and has been living at the

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.130
very bottom of the pit.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:27.030
Goreng arrives there after riding the platform
down with a weapon, enforcing the rationing

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:31.040
of the food by beating and killing anyone
who tries to take more than their share.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.040
His plan is to return back to the top level
with an uneaten dessert, a symbol that the

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.930
prisoners have learned how to work together
to survive in such a way.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:43.550
However, after finding the child, Goreng realizes
that that isn't the message he needs to send.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:48.319
The discovery of Miharu's child is confirmation
of the most important component of the VSC

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:52.750
experiment: those in power set the rules,
but they clearly don't follow them themselves.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.470
Although they've assured everyone they would
never do something as cruel as allow a child

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.210
into such a horrendous existence, that was
a lie.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:03.099
The conclusion of The Platform sees Goreng
sending the child back up on the platform

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:07.410
as a message to the chefs at the top, who
are presumably in a similar state of ignorance

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.410
as Imoguiri.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.940
Instead of proving to them that the prisoners
can adapt to the system, he wants to show

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.650
them that the system itself is unspeakably
and unfathomably cruel, and those operating

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.070
it cannot be trusted.

